{"about":"0f7a8b30a80188d2b98cfe5cccc160f1b2c70b259fbc2ab374c714f493c4a6bd","body":"The conjecture suggests that relying on 'evolutionary elimination of error' smuggles in induction or leaves us without a defensible basis for action. However, this misses Popper's core argument that the preference for better-corroborated theories is not a prediction that they will remain true (which would be inductive), but a rational choice based on the fact that they have withstood attempts to falsify them where others failed. The 'cost' described is actually the strength of the position: it admits no guarantee of future success, thereby avoiding the logical trap of induction while still providing a robust, critical rationale for why we should act on the best-tested theory available.","kind":"mini.criticism.v1","named":"The alleged circularity of relying on evolutionary elimination"}